 "productionNotes": "No single production total is given for this generation because the published figures are series totals by model year, they disagree on what they include, and none of them comes from a Chevrolet or General Motors document fetched for this page. The year-by-year Impala figures here come from the tables on 348-409.com, an enthusiast site that lists 491,000 Impalas for 1961, 704,900 for 1962, 832,600 for 1963 and 889,600 for 1964 under the label Impala (all engines), and states that its numbers were collected from various sources with no guarantee of accuracy. A catalog-style reprint on oldride.com gives the same 704,900 for 1962 but labels it a total excluding station wagons, so whether the 1962 figure counts the Impala wagon is not settled by these two sources. Body-style rows on 348-409.com, such as 177,969 two-door Sport Coupes for 1961, cover every full-size Chevrolet series, not the Impala alone. The Super Sport is counted differently again. For 1961 three sources agree on 453 SS cars, about 142 of them with the 409. For 1962 oldride.com and 348-409.com agree on 99,311 cars with the SS option. For 1963, 153,271. For 1964, when the SS became its own series, 348-409.com lists 119,166 and musclecarclub.com lists 185,325, an unresolved gap of 66,159 cars. The only per-year 409 counts found, 15,091 SS 409s for 1962 and 8,684 409 Impalas for 1964, come from one enthusiast site and are carried at low confidence. No Chevrolet press material, brochure or GM Heritage document was retrieved for this page, so spec 4a category 1 rests on catalog reprints rather than the manufacturer, and the only US factory prices documented here are 1964 list prices from the oldride.com reprint. No US MSRP for a 1961-1963 Impala body was documented in the sources consulted.",
